.TH "ELEMENT.KEYUP_EVENT" "3JS" "September 4, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.keyup_event \- Element: keyup event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]keyup\f[B]\f[R] event is fired when a key is released.
.PP
The \f[CR]keydown\f[R] and \f[CR]keyup\f[R] events provide a code
indicating which key is pressed, while \f[CR]keypress\f[R] indicates
which character was entered.
For example, a lowercase \(lqa\(rq will be reported as 65 by
\f[CR]keydown\f[R] and \f[CR]keyup\f[R], but as 97 by
\f[CR]keypress\f[R].
An uppercase \(lqA\(rq is reported as 65 by all events.
.PP
The event target of a key event is the currently focused element which
is processing the keyboard activity.
This includes: \f[CR]<input>\f[R], \f[CR]<textarea>\f[R], anything that
is \f[CR]contentEditable\f[R], and anything else that can be interacted
with the keyboard, such as \f[CR]<a>\f[R], \f[CR]<button>\f[R], and
\f[CR]<summary>\f[R].
If no suitable element is in focus, the event target will be the
\f[CR]<body>\f[R] or the root.
The event bubbles.
It can reach \f[CR]Document\f[R] and \f[CR]Window\f[R].
.PP
The event target might change between different key events.
For example, the \f[CR]keydown\f[R] target for pressing the Tab key
would be different from the \f[CR]keyup\f[R] target, because the focus
has changed.
.PP
Because the event fires after the key is released, modifier\-key
properties (\f[CR]ctrlKey\f[R], \f[CR]altKey\f[R], \f[CR]shiftKey\f[R],
and \f[CR]metaKey\f[R]) reflect the state after release.
For example, \f[CR]ctrlKey\f[R] is \f[CR]false\f[R] on the
\f[CR]keyup\f[R] event for the Control key itself.
If another key is released while the Control key remains pressed,
\f[CR]ctrlKey\f[R] is \f[CR]true\f[R] on that key\(cqs \f[CR]keyup\f[R]
event.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqkeyup\(dq, (event) => { })

onkeyup = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]KeyboardEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]UIEvent\f[R] and \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS addEventListener keyup example
This example logs the \f[CR]KeyboardEvent.code\f[R] value whenever you
release a key inside the \f[CR]<input>\f[R] element.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]input\f[R] placeholder=\(dqClick here, then press and release a key.\(dq size=\(dq40\(dq />
<\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqlog\(dq></\f[B]p\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] input = document.querySelector(\(dqinput\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] log = document.getElementById(\(dqlog\(dq);

input.addEventListener(\(dqkeyup\(dq, logKey);

\f[B]function\f[R] logKey(e) {
  log.textContent += \(ga ${e.code}\(ga;
}
.EE
.SS keyup events with IME
Since Firefox 65, the \f[CR]keydown\f[R] and \f[CR]keyup\f[R] events are
now fired during Input method editor composition, to improve
cross\-browser compatibility for CJKT users (\c
.UR https://bugzil.la/354358
Firefox bug 354358
.UE \c
).
To ignore all \f[CR]keyup\f[R] events that are part of composition, do
something like this:
.IP
.EX
eventTarget.addEventListener(\(dqkeyup\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]if\f[R] (event.isComposing) {
    \f[B]return\f[R];
  }
  \f[I]// do something\f[R]
});
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Unlike \f[CR]keydown\f[R], \f[CR]keyup\f[R] events do
not have special \f[CR]keyCode\f[R] values for IME events.
However, like \f[CR]keydown\f[R], \f[CR]compositionstart\f[R] may fire
\f[I]after\f[R] \f[CR]keyup\f[R] when typing the first character that
opens up the IME, and \f[CR]compositionend\f[R] may fire
\f[I]before\f[R] \f[CR]keyup\f[R] when typing the last character that
closes the IME.
In these cases, \f[CR]isComposing\f[R] is false even when the event is
part of composition.
.RE
